          "option_id": "e1f660bc-27df-4d23-8940-72db52ee93a3",
          "text": "Pathological possession",
          "is_correct": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "option_id": "db38cb4f-fb52-4094-852e-effa3fcc8313",
          "text": "Herbal products",
          "is_correct": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "option_id": "6fbf214a-3ddf-43f8-b4a6-438ae52dbe5f",
          "text": "Normative spiritual and religious practices",
          "is_correct": false
        }
      ],
      "correct_answer": {
        "letter": "B",
        "option_id": "e1f660bc-27df-4d23-8940-72db52ee93a3",
        "text": "Pathological possession"
      },
      "correct_answers": [
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "option_id": "e1f660bc-27df-4d23-8940-72db52ee93a3",
          "text": "Pathological possession",
          "is_correct": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "ANSWER: B The DSM-5 includes pathological possession. It is a disorder of identity alteration that occurs during an altered state of consciousness. It is attributed to possession by an external spirit, power, deity, or other person.",
      "explanation_images": [],
      "explanation_image_url": null
    },
    {
      "question_number": 2,
      "question_id": "dfb1fcbc-4978-4230-87b9-3681aa295ad8",
      "question_type": "MULTIPLE_CHOICE",
      "difficulty": "MEDIUM",
      "question_text": "Which of the following is considered a “positive” dissociative symptom?",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "image_url": "",
      "options": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "option_id": "1715b759-897e-4b90-8840-85422371ee3b",
          "text": "Derealization",
          "is_correct": true
        },
        {
          "letter": "B",
          "option_id": "272733b9-2d27-45f2-a0d6-a75abe6b71fe",
          "text": "Amnesia",
          "is_correct": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "C",
          "option_id": "ba9f9cc5-b41e-491e-b350-e3378869a103",
          "text": "Aphonia",
          "is_correct": false
        },
        {
          "letter": "D",
          "option_id": "accac2c4-b98e-435a-b590-f1f7fbb168ee",
          "text": "Paralysis",
          "is_correct": false
        }
      ],
      "correct_answer": {
        "letter": "A",
        "option_id": "1715b759-897e-4b90-8840-85422371ee3b",
        "text": "Derealization"
      },
      "correct_answers": [
        {
          "letter": "A",
          "option_id": "1715b759-897e-4b90-8840-85422371ee3b",
          "text": "Derealization",
          "is_correct": true
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "ANSWER: A Dissociative symptoms can affect all psychological functions and are usually divided into positive and negative symptoms. Positive symptoms include intrusions into awareness and behavior, with accompanying deficits in continuity of subjective experience (e.g., identity fragmentation, depersonalization, derealization). Negative symptoms are the inability to access information or to control mental functions (e.g., amnesia, aphonia, paralysis).",
      "explanation_images": [],
      "explanation_image_url": null
    },
    {
